 interest and additional signals called the background. Here we focus on the problem of measuring infrared spectra emitted by interstellar clouds. The signals of interest are infrared emissions from polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs), which are a class of complex organic molecules. The PAH emissions are characterized by emission bands near 3.3, 6.2, 7.7, 8.6, 11.2, and 15-20 microns. The background consists of a host of associated spectral signals which, in the simplest case, can include emissions from multiple Planck blackbodies as well as broadband and narrowband emissions. To analyze the PAH spectra we must accurately assess this background. To do this, we have developed a Bayesian algorithm based on nested sampling (Skilling 2005, Sivia & Skilling 2006). The spectral model consists of a mixture of Planck functions and Gaussians. We demonstrate this algorithm on both synthetic data and infrared spectra recorded from interstellar clouds. The result shows that the algorithm can accurately identify and remove simple backgrounds. In future work, we plan to incorporate mixtures of PAH spectra and more complex models …[truncated]", "text_truncated": true}, {"line": 10, "text": ""}], "document_id": "5cc6879681c1e6a9bd9733080771fc39", "doi": "10.1063/1.2821278", "line": 9, "path": "parquet:///mnt/nas/data/gaojing/markdown_corpus_v1/packages/markdown-v1-final/publisher_group%3Daip_publishing/part-00000.parquet#5cc6879681c1e6a9bd9733080771fc39", "publisher_group": "aip_publishing", "synthetic": false, "text": "Abstract. All measurements consist of a mixture of the signal of interest and additional signals called the background. Here we focus on the problem of measuring infrared spectra emitted by interstellar clouds. The signals of interest are infrared emissions from polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs), which are a class of complex organic molecules. The PAH emissions are characterized by emission bands near 3.3, 6.2, 7.7, 8.6, 11.2, and 15-20 microns. The background consists of a host of associated spectral signals which, in the simplest case, can include emissions from multiple Planck blackbodies as well as broadband and narrowband emissions. To analyze the PAH spectra we must accurately assess this background. To do this, we have developed a Bayesian algorithm based on nested sampling (Skilling 2005, Sivia & Skilling 2006). The spectral model consists of a mixture of Planck functions and Gaussians. We demonstrate this algorithm on both synthetic data and infrared spectra recorded from interstellar clouds. The result shows that the algorithm can accurately identify and remove simple backgrounds. In future work, we plan to incorporate mixtures of PAH spectra and more complex models …[truncated]", "text_truncated": true, "title": "Estimating Background Spectra"}, {"citable": true, "context": [{"line": 177, "text": "28A. Zakharova, M. Kapeller, and E. Sch€oll, Phys. Rev. Lett. 112, 154101   "}, {"line": 178, "text": "(2014). 29A. Yeldesbay, A. Pikovsky, and M. Rosenblum, Phys. Rev. Lett. 112, 144103 (2014).   "}, {"line": 179, "text": "30L. Schmidt and K. Krischer, Phys. Rev. Lett. 114, 034101 (2015).   "}], "document_id": "b0637de6683f6af49669a709b1a1d2c5", "doi": "10.1063/1.4927829", "line": 178, "path": "parquet:///mnt/nas/data/gaojing/markdown_corpus_v1/packages/markdown-v1-final/publisher_group%3Daip_publishing/part-00000.parquet#b0637de6683f6af49669a709b1a1d2c5", "publisher_group": "aip_publishing", "synthetic": false, "text": "(2014). 29A. Yeldesbay, A. Pikovsky, and M. Rosenblum, Phys. Rev. Lett. 112, 144103 (2014).   ", "title": "Nonlinearity of local dynamics promotes multi-chimeras"}, {"citable": true, "context": [{"line": 59, "text": ""}, {"line": 60, "text": "ately 300 km (Titan IV(SRMU)/NUS to LEO). This is the same mass as delivered to GEO by the Titan IV(SRMU)/Centaur launcher but a cost savings of up to \\$125M per launch is generated by saving the Centaur stages and the additional handling and integration needed (Bayer, 193). There are intermediate py-"}, {"line": 61, "text": ""}], "document_id": "a36bafd0bc24baa48386a4ec7ff09b1b", "doi": "10.1063/1.2950263", "line": 60, "path": "parquet:///mnt/nas/data/gaojing/markdown_corpus_v1/packages/markdown-v1-final/publisher_group%3Daip_publishing/part-00000.parquet#a36bafd0bc24baa48386a4ec7ff09b1b", "publisher_group": "aip_publishing", "synthetic": false, "text": "ately 300 km (Titan IV(SRMU)/NUS to LEO). This is the same mass as delivered to GEO by the Titan IV(SRMU)/Centaur launcher but a cost savings of up to \\$125M per launch is generated by saving the Centaur stages and the additional handling and integration needed (Bayer, 193). There are intermediate py-", "title": "Summary of the Autonomous Transport System Study"}], "ok": true, "parquet_files": 15, "query": "BaY", "roots": ["/mnt/nas/data/gaojing/markdown_corpus_v1/packages/markdown-v1-final"], "timed_out": false, "timeout_seconds": 30.0, "total_returned": 5, "truncated": true}, "run_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-09-03T03:15:06.002793+00:00", "tool": "grep_local_corpus"}
